--TEST--
A request carries one body, and which of the four keys it was written under is what says how it is framed and what type it is sent as — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *invariance over a sweep*: four calls differing only in which
// body key they wrote, asserted by walking the records rather than by reading
// one of them, so a framing that answers one key plausibly and sends another
// under the wrong type still fails here. Nothing below is granted `net.connect`
// and no socket is opened — the table answers every call, and what a record
// holds is the framing a real call would have written
// (`rule:testing/an-outbound-call-is-answered-from-a-table`).
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/*", 200, {body: "ok"});

// `json` is the value as a document, encoded once for the call: an object stays
// an object rather than being sniffed out of an array of strings, which is why
// there are four keys and no mode beside one of them
// (`rule:core-api/no-mode-strings`).
var $json = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/json", {json: ["name" => "ada", "active" => true]});
// `form` percent-encodes the same map under the encoding its name gives, where
// a space is `+` and `+` is not a space.
var $form = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/form", {form: ["name" => "ada lovelace", "note" => "a+b"]});
// `body` sends the octets as given, under the `contentType` that types them and
// means nothing without them.
var $raw = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/raw", {body: "the octets as given", contentType: "text/plain"});
// `multipart` frames a map as segments, and a `Core\Http\Part` is the arm of a
// field that carries a name and a type of its own.
var $multipart = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/multipart", {multipart: [
    "name" => "ada",
    "avatar" => Core\Http\Part::bytes("PNG.", "avatar.png", {contentType: "image/png"}),
]});

// One line per call, read off the record rather than off the option: the type
// is the framing's own answer and the program wrote none of the four. A
// multipart boundary is drawn per call, so what is asserted about that one is
// the part of the header that cannot vary and the length, which the boundary's
// fixed width leaves fixed.
var $sent = Core\Test::sentHttp();
echo Core\Arr::count($sent), "\n";
foreach ($sent as Core\Test\SentRequest $call) {
    string $type = $call->header("content-type") ?? "-";
    if (Core\Str::startsWith($type, "multipart/form-data; boundary=----NovisBoundary")) {
        $type = "multipart/form-data, boundary drawn per call";
    }
    echo $call->url(), " ", $type, " ", Core\Bytes::length($call->body()), "\n";
}

// The three whose framing is fixed, byte for byte.
foreach ($sent as Core\Test\SentRequest $call) {
    if (!Core\Str::startsWith($call->header("content-type") ?? "-", "multipart/")) {
        echo ($call->body() as string), "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
4
https://api.example.com/json application/json 28
https://api.example.com/form application/x-www-form-urlencoded 28
https://api.example.com/raw text/plain 19
https://api.example.com/multipart multipart/form-data, boundary drawn per call 316
{"name":"ada","active":true}
name=ada+lovelace&note=a%2Bb
the octets as given
